  <!-- Section 5: Polyester Blends -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Polyester Blends | Combining Polyester with Other Fibers</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Polyester is frequently blended with other fibers to combine the advantages of multiple materials. Blends offer performance characteristics that neither fiber provides alone. Understanding common polyester blends helps you select fabrics optimized for your specific requirements.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Polyester-Cotton Blends</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Polyester-cotton blends are the most common polyester blend, typically combining 65% polyester with 35% cotton, though ratios vary. The blend combines polyester's durability, wrinkle resistance, and color retention with cotton's comfort, breathability, and natural feel. Poly-cotton is softer and more comfortable than pure polyester while more durable and easier to care for than pure cotton. This blend is widely used for casual shirts, uniforms, workwear, and everyday garments. The 65/35 ratio provides a balanced performance, but other ratios like 50/50 offer different characteristics. SDF sources poly-cotton fabrics in various weights and constructions.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Polyester-Spandex Blends</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Polyester-spandex blends (also called elastane or Lycra) add stretch and recovery to polyester. Spandex content typically ranges from 5-20% depending on the desired stretch level. These blends are essential for activewear, leggings, swimwear, and form-fitting garments where movement and fit are critical. Polyester provides durability and moisture-wicking properties while spandex adds four-way stretch and shape retention. The combination creates performance fabrics comfortable for active use. Our <a href="/activewear-manufacturer">activewear manufacturing</a> extensively uses polyester-spandex blends for optimal performance.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Polyester-Rayon Blends</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Polyester-rayon blends combine polyester's strength with rayon's drape and softness. Rayon is a semi-synthetic fiber made from regenerated cellulose, offering a luxurious feel similar to silk. The blend creates fabrics that drape beautifully while maintaining durability. Used for dresses, blouses, and flowing garments where drape and appearance are priorities. Polyester-rayon blends are more affordable than pure rayon while offering similar aesthetic properties with better durability.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Polyester-Wool Blends</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Polyester-wool blends add durability and reduce wool's tendency to felt and shrink. Wool provides warmth and natural luxury feel while polyester adds strength and easy care. These blends are used for suits, jackets, and winter wear where wool's warmth is desired but pure wool's care requirements are impractical. The blend maintains wool's appearance while improving garment longevity and reducing maintenance. Polyester content typically ranges from 20-50% depending on the desired balance of properties.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Polyester-Linen Blends</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Polyester-linen blends reduce linen's characteristic wrinkling while maintaining its natural appearance and breathability. Linen provides crisp texture and natural aesthetic while polyester adds wrinkle resistance and durability. These blends are used for summer clothing, casual shirts, and warm-weather garments where linen's appearance is desired without its high-maintenance nature. The blend retains linen's casual, natural look while improving garment care and longevity.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-wrapper">
        <table class="comparison" role="table" aria-label="Polyester blend types and their characteristics">
