  <!-- Section 6: Recycled Polyester -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Recycled Polyester (rPET) | Sustainable Polyester Option</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Recycled polyester, commonly abbreviated rPET, is polyester made from recycled materials rather than virgin petroleum. rPET addresses environmental concerns associated with virgin polyester while maintaining performance properties. For brands prioritizing sustainability, rPET offers a way to use polyester with reduced environmental impact.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">How rPET Is Made</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        rPET is primarily made from recycled plastic bottles (PET bottles). The bottles are collected, cleaned, sorted, and shredded into flakes. The flakes are melted and extruded into polyester fiber. The resulting rPET fiber has identical chemical properties to virgin polyester and can be used in the same applications. rPET can also be made from post-industrial polyester waste or recycled polyester garments. The recycling process diverts plastic waste from landfills and reduces demand for virgin petroleum. GRS (Global Recycled Standard) certification verifies recycled content and responsible production practices.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Environmental Benefits of rPET</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        rPET offers significant environmental advantages over virgin polyester. Production requires approximately 50% less energy than virgin polyester, reducing greenhouse gas emissions. rPET uses recycled materials instead of petroleum, conserving non-renewable resources. The process diverts plastic bottles from landfills and oceans, addressing plastic waste problems. rPET maintains polyester's performance properties - durability, wrinkle resistance, color retention - so sustainability doesn't require sacrificing quality. For brands with sustainability commitments, rPET enables polyester use with reduced environmental footprint. Learn more about our <a href="/sustainability/">sustainability initiatives</a> and recycled material options.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">rPET Certifications</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        When sourcing rPET, verify certifications to ensure recycled content claims are legitimate. GRS (Global Recycled Standard) is the leading certification, verifying recycled content, chain of custody, and environmental/social practices. RCS (Recycled Claim Standard) is a simpler certification verifying recycled content without the full GRS requirements. OEKO-TEX certification ensures rPET fabrics are free from harmful chemicals. SDF provides GRS-certified rPET options for brands requiring verified sustainable materials. Our <a href="/certifications">certifications</a> include GRS for recycled polyester.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">rPET vs Virgin Polyester</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        rPET has identical performance properties to virgin polyester - same durability, wrinkle resistance, color retention, and care requirements. The difference is environmental, not functional. rPET may have slightly higher cost due to recycling processes, but the gap is narrowing as recycling scales. rPET can be recycled again, though quality degrades with each recycling cycle. For most applications, rPET is a direct substitute for virgin polyester with sustainability benefits. Brands can transition to rPET without changing garment specifications or performance expectations.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 7: FAQ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Polyester Clothing Manufacturing FAQ</h2>

      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is polyester good for clothing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, polyester is excellent for many clothing applications. It offers durability, wrinkle resistance, color retention, quick-drying properties, and affordability. Polyester is ideal for activewear, outdoor clothing, uniforms, and everyday garments where performance and easy care matter. Modern polyester fabrics have improved breathability and comfort compared to older versions through moisture-wicking treatments and engineered textures. Blends like polyester-cotton combine polyester's durability with cotton's comfort for balanced performance. SDF produces high-quality polyester garments suitable for diverse applications from performance apparel to casual wear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the difference between polyester and poly-cotton?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Polyester is 100% synthetic made from petroleum-derived chemicals, while poly-cotton is a blend combining polyester and cotton fibers, typically in 65/35 or 50/50 ratios. 100% polyester offers maximum durability, wrinkle resistance, and quick-drying properties but can feel less breathable. Poly-cotton blends provide a balance - polyester adds strength and wrinkle resistance, cotton adds comfort and breathability. Poly-cotton is softer and more comfortable against the skin than pure polyester but slightly less durable. Poly-cotton also requires more care than pure polyester (more wrinkling, more shrinkage). Choose pure polyester for maximum performance and durability, poly-cotton for balanced comfort and performance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Is polyester clothing bad for the environment?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Polyester has legitimate environmental concerns. It's made from petroleum, a non-renewable resource, and production generates greenhouse gases. Polyester sheds microplastics during washing - tiny synthetic fibers that pollute waterways and marine ecosystems. Polyester doesn't biodegrade quickly, contributing to long-term waste. However, polyester's durability means garments last longer, potentially reducing consumption frequency. Recycled polyester (rPET) made from plastic bottles significantly reduces environmental impact by using recycled materials instead of virgin petroleum. rPET production requires approximately 50% less energy than virgin polyester. Our <a href="/sustainability/">sustainability initiatives</a> include rPET options and responsible sourcing to mitigate environmental impact.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Does polyester shrink when washed?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Polyester has minimal shrinkage compared to natural fibers like cotton or wool. Polyester typically shrinks less than 3% during washing, which is barely noticeable and often within normal fit tolerance. This low shrinkage is one of polyester's advantages - garments maintain size and shape over many washes. However, polyester can shrink slightly if washed in hot water or dried at high temperatures. To minimize any potential shrinkage, wash polyester in cold or warm water (not hot) and tumble dry on low heat or air dry. Polyester's low shrinkage means pre-shrinking during manufacturing is rarely necessary, unlike cotton which often requires pre-shrinking processes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What types of polyester fabric are used in clothing?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Common polyester fabric types include: Polyester taffeta (lightweight, crisp, used for linings and lightweight jackets), polyester crepe (textured surface, drapes well, used for dresses and blouses), polyester satin (smooth, glossy surface, used for evening wear and linings), polyester chiffon (sheer, lightweight, used for overlays and decorative applications), polyester jersey (knit fabric with stretch, used for t-shirts and activewear), and polyester fleece (soft, insulating, used for jackets and hoodies). Each type has specific properties suited to different applications - taffeta for structure, crepe for drape, satin for luxury, chiffon for ethereal quality, jersey for comfort and stretch, fleece for warmth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Can polyester be blended with other fabrics?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, polyester is commonly blended with other fibers to combine properties from multiple materials. Polyester-cotton blends (65/35 or 50/50) combine durability with comfort. Polyester-spandex blends (95/5 to 80/20) add stretch for activewear and form-fitting garments. Polyester-rayon blends offer drape and softness while maintaining durability. Polyester-wool blends add durability and reduce wool's tendency to felt and shrink. Polyester-linen blends reduce wrinkling while maintaining linen's natural appearance. SDF can source various polyester blends based on your specific performance and comfort requirements. Blends allow you to tailor fabric properties to your exact needs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How long do polyester clothes last?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Polyester clothing is highly durable and typically lasts 5-10 years with regular use if cared for properly. Polyester resists abrasion, tearing, pilling, and fading better than many natural fibers including cotton and wool. The synthetic fibers have high tensile strength and maintain shape over time. Polyester's color retention means garments maintain appearance without fading from sunlight or washing. Polyester doesn't biodegrade quickly, which contributes to longevity but also environmental persistence. With proper care - gentle washing in cold or warm water, avoiding high heat drying - polyester garments can last significantly longer than cotton equivalents. This durability makes polyester cost-effective over the garment lifecycle despite potentially higher initial cost for some applications.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is recycled polyester (rPET)?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Recycled polyester (rPET) is polyester made from recycled materials rather than virgin petroleum. The primary source is recycled plastic bottles (PET bottles). Bottles are collected, cleaned, shredded into flakes, melted, and extruded into new polyester fiber. rPET has identical chemical properties and performance characteristics to virgin polyester - same durability, wrinkle resistance, color retention, and care requirements. The environmental benefit comes from using recycled materials instead of virgin petroleum, reducing energy consumption by approximately 50% and diverting plastic waste from landfills. rPET can be certified through GRS (Global Recycled Standard) to verify recycled content. SDF offers rPET options for brands seeking sustainable materials. rPET is increasingly used in activewear, outdoor clothing, and sustainable fashion lines.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

